#{[17592186596670 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem applies to system I. The numbers $3,$ $5,$ and $7$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 5\\cdot 7=105.$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $5$ and $21$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $5\\cdot 21=105.$\n\n- The theorem applies to system III. The numbers $7$ and $15$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $7\\cdot 15=105.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186596722 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem applies to system I. The numbers $5,$ $6,$ and $7$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $5\\cdot 6\\cdot 7=210.$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $10$ and $21$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $10\\cdot 21=210.$\n\n- The theorem applies to system III. The numbers $14$ and $15$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $14\\cdot 15=210.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186599790 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+1){y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =y\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186599805 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(x-1)({e}^{y}-1)}{x{e}^{y}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186599835 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ x, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>1 \\\\ y, & x>1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186599842 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-6x})(1-{e}^{-y/3}) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-y/3}-{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-6x-y/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-y/3}-{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-6x-y/3}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601938 "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=1.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=1\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186604566 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=32\\%=0.32,$ and $n=45.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =32\\%=0.32, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{45} \\\\  & ≈0.0048\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186604573 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=10\\%=0.1,\\,n=90.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(90,0.1)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =90\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =90\\cdot 0.1\\cdot (1-0.1) \\\\  & =8.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186604625 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=30\\%=0.3,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =30\\%=0.3, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{10} \\\\  & =0.021.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605834 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2e7544d7b54cf31fccb1b445688a26a5f8fcaf23201a32dc4ee2a8d2b09b6ce.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}6xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1} \\\\  & =3{[x{y}^{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1} \\\\  & =3[x\\cdot {1}^{2}-x\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =3[x-{x}^{5}] \\\\  & =3x-3{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3{x}^{5}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605866 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{36}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{108}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}{x}^{3}({3}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le \\sqrt{5}) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{5}}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}[{2}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605884 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2x\\}.$\n\n![](images/e1/e18840d628c889acb89d5c8815324e050cf1bb20e856a859e20a9164fcd81b5e.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{1}{4}y{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{-x}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[{y}^{2}{e}^{-x}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[(2x)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-x}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-x}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[4{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605885 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{56}t(5-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{3}{56}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{56}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{112}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{56}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{15{x}^{2}}{112}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{56}, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605891 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[xy]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2y-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{u}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{y}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1 & y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$"] [17592186605905 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/e6/e63b557f30a1a54e5fbaea8999dd420deec67f4a0ed770dc1ada48affb7c9be7.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{x}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{[xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x} \\\\  & ={[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x} \\\\  & =(2x\\cdot x+{x}^{2})-(2x\\cdot 0+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605910 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}3{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}3{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-6x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-6x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-6x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605925 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{t}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186607567 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[1,9],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(4<X\\le 9) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9-4}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186607571 "Let's number each alphabet letter according to their standard order and let $X$ represent the number of the chosen letter. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,26\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\in \\{1,5,9,15,21\\}).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,26\\},$ and the set $S$ has $|S|=26$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{26}, & x=1,2,…,26 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\in \\{1,5,9,15,21\\}$ are $1,5,9,15,21.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\in \\{1,5,9,15,21\\})=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}=\\frac{5}{26}.\n$$"] [17592186607586 "Let $X$ represent the number on which the die lands. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{10,11,…,17\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\ge 15).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{10,11,…,17\\},$ and the set $S=\\{10,11,…,17\\}$ has $|S|=17-10+1=8$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}, & x=10,11,…,17 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\ge 15$ are $15,16,17.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 15)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186607589 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[2,8],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 8, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<6) & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6-3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186607596 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[2,7],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & 2\\le w\\le 7, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\ge \\sqrt{5}) & =P(\\sqrt{5}\\le W\\le 7) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{5}}^{7}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{5}}^{7}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{7-\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186607617 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[2,5],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 7) & =P(4<X\\le 5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5-4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186609832 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =0.984375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.984375 \\\\  & ≈0.0156.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186609846 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.83),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.17)^{x-1}(0.83), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.17)^{0}(0.83)+(0.17)^{1}(0.83)+(0.17)^{2}(0.83) \\\\  & ≈0.9951.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186609873 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.5)}^{x-1}(0.5), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & ={(0.5)}^{1}(0.5)+{(0.5)}^{2}(0.5)+{(0.5)}^{3}(0.5)+{(0.5)}^{4}(0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.4688.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186609897 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{3}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{544}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{544}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186612428 "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186612429 "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x-1.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y<x+2 \\\\ y>-x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186612430 "Consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2x.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $x+2y=0.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+2y<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 2x \\\\ x+2y<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613134 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-4I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-4I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-4I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-4I$ is singular, then $(A-4I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186613147 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have a line with a slope of $-1$ and $y$-intercept $4.$ So the graph is the line $y=-x+4$ that ends at $(1,3),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have a line with a slope of $1$ that passes through the point $(1,1).$ We can find the equation of this line using point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-1=1(x-1)\\,⟹\\,y=x\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=x$ that starts at $(1,1),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613203 "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have $f(x)=2.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=-2.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613736 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x/9}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(10\\le X<15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10\\le X<15) & ={\\int }_{10}^{15}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{15}\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/9}{|}_{10}^{15} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5/3}+{e}^{-10/9} \\\\  & ≈0.1403\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613751 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $-1\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+1\\cdot {y}^{2}-(-\\frac{1}{3}-{y}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{2}{3}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{y}{4}+\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{16}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{16})-(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{16}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613800 "The marginal density function for $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186613802 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(1.5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1.5{e}^{-1.5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X\\ge 1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }1.5{e}^{-1.5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1.5x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-1.5} \\\\  & ≈0.2231\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186614589 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{9}(5-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(\\frac{10}{9}t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9})|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-10x+16) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-8)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-8)(x-2), & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186616956 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col)}(A)=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null)}(A)=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. A matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=0,$ our matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is *not* invertible, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible,  it must be $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186617001 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${R}^{n}.$"] [17592186617003 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $Ax=0$ has no non-trivial solution.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\},$ which means that $Ax=0$ has no non-trivial solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186617034 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ is invertible because $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since,\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186617780 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-5,1],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & -5\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<Y<0) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{0-(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617787 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-3,0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\, & -3\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =P(-3\\le X\\le -1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617795 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,-5],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & -10\\le x\\le -5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-9<X\\le -6) & ={\\int }_{-9}^{-6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-9}^{-6}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-(-9)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617802 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-5,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15},\\, & -5\\le y\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 0) & =P(0\\le Y\\le 10) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{10}\\frac{1}{15}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{10-0}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617807 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-2,5],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7},\\, & -2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =P(1<X<5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617826 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -6\\le x\\le -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-5\\le X<-3) & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-3}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-(-5)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617842 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\le w\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\le 1) & =P(-\\frac{3}{2}\\le W\\le 1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/2}^{1}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617847 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[1,2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le \\sqrt{3}) & =P(1\\le Y\\le \\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622783 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}(y-1)}{y}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622811 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{1-x})(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-{e}^{1-x}+\\frac{{e}^{1-x}}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-{e}^{1-x}+\\frac{{e}^{1-x}}{{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186627687 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $det(A)=0.$* $\\,✓$  \n\nSince $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible, which means $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186627692 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\},$ which means that $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186627711 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.6),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{2}(0.6)+(0.4)^{3}(0.6)+(0.4)^{4}(0.6) \\\\  & =0.14976.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186627712 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.34),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.66)^{x-1}(0.34), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<6) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.66)^{1}(0.34)+(0.66)^{2}(0.34)+(0.66)^{3}(0.34)+(0.66)^{4}(0.34) \\\\  & ≈0.5348.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186627715 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{nullity}(A).$"] [17592186627728 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.9630.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186627730 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{2}(0.3) \\\\  & =0.147.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186631883 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=19 \\\\ (\\,-x+4y=10\\,)\\,\\times (2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y &=19 \\\\ -2x+8y &=20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2x}+5y & =19 \\\\ -\\cancel{2x}+8y & =20 \\\\ \\hline (5y+8y) & =(19+20) \\\\ 13y & =39 \\\\ y & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =10 \\\\ -x+4(3) & =10 \\\\ -x+12 & =10 \\\\ -x & =-2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(2,3).$"] [17592186631897 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $3$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-9y=8 \\\\ (\\,2x+3y=4\\,)\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-9y &=8 \\\\ 6x+9y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}6x-\\cancel{9y} & =8 \\\\ 6x+\\cancel{9y} & =12 \\\\ \\hline (6x+6x) & =(8+12) \\\\ 12x & =20\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $12x.$"] [17592186631950 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3x+2y=3\\,)\\,\\times (-4) \\\\ 12x+7y=6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x-8y &=-12 \\\\ 12x+7y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{12x}-8y & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{12x}+7y & =6 \\\\ \\hline (-8y+7y) & =(-12+6) \\\\ -y & =-6\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=6.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=6$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y & =3 \\\\ 3x+2(6) & =3 \\\\ 3x+12 & =3 \\\\ 3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=6,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,6).$"] [17592186631963 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-3y=1 \\\\ (\\,-2x+5y=4\\,)\\,\\times (4)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-3y &=1 \\\\ -8x+20y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{8x}-3y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{8x}+20y & =16 \\\\ \\hline (-3y+20y) & =(1+16) \\\\ 17y & =17\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186631968 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4a-3b=6\\,)\\,\\times (4) \\\\ 5a+12b=-5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16a-12b &=24 \\\\ 5a+12b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}16a-\\cancel{12b} & =24 \\\\ 5a+\\cancel{12b} & =-5 \\\\ \\hline (16a+5a) & =(24-5) \\\\ 21a & =19\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $21a.$"] [17592186631970 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $7$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}24x-14y=22 \\\\ (\\,4x+2y=-5\\,)\\,\\times (7)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}24x-14y &=22 \\\\ 28x+14y &=-35\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}24x-\\cancel{14y} & =22 \\\\ 28x+\\cancel{14y} & =-35 \\\\ \\hline (24x+28x) & =(22-35) \\\\ 52x & =-13\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $52x.$"] [17592186631971 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4t+2v=-1\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 3t-4v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8t+4v &=-2 \\\\ 3t-4v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}8t+\\cancel{4v} & =-2 \\\\ 3t-\\cancel{4v} & =1 \\\\ \\hline (8t+3t) & =(-2+1) \\\\ 11t & =-1\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $11t.$"] [17592186635493 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-(x+y)}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-(x+0)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le X<10) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{10}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{10}{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-x}]}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186635497 "An exponential random variable $W$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nW∼\\text{Exp}(0.5).\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.5{e}^{-0.5w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(2\\le W\\le 4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le W\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}0.5{e}^{-0.5w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.5w}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.2325\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186646269 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,19\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(7<X\\le 10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,19\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,19\\}$ has $|S|=19-5+1=15$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15}, & x=5,6,…,19 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $7<X\\le 10$ are $8,9,10.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(7<X\\le 10)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{15}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186651596 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{b,c,d\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4afa52dee6ffc5c42d95d684871dbedfb1445bbce5eabc6b9319e97ae6dafd2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $b,c$ and $d$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$"] [17592186651613 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $0,4,6$ and $8$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{0,4,6,8\\}.$"] [17592186652257 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,8),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,8), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-8,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-x){e}^{7(-x)^{2}+2} \\\\  & =x{e}^{7{x}^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2){e}^{7(-2)^{2}+2}=-2{e}^{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186653539 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20},\\, & -10\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X<4) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186653544 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\sqrt{2},0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\, & -\\sqrt{2}\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge -1) & =P(-1\\le X\\le 0) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(0-(-1))\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186653550 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-1) & =P(-\\frac{9}{2}\\le X<-1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-9/2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-9/2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{[-1-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)]}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186653554 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[4,7],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\, & 4\\le x\\le 7, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X<10) & =P(5\\le x\\le 7) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7-5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654263 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]$, we have a line with a slope of $1$ that passes through the point $(-1,0).$ We can find the equation of this line using point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-0=1(x-(-1))\\,⟹\\,y=x+1\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=x+1$ that ends at $(-1,0),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(-1,\\infty ),$ we have a line with a slope of $-1$ and $y$-intercept $1.$ So the graph is the line $y=-x+1$ that starts at $(-1,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+1,\\, & x\\le -1 \\\\ -x+1,\\, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654276 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3t}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654290 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,3],$ we have a line with a slope of $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3.$ So the graph is the line $y=-x+3$ that ends at $(3,0),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(3,\\infty ),$ we have a horizontal line that passes through the point $(3,3).$ So the graph is the line $y=3$ that starts at $(3,3),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+3,\\, & x\\le 3 \\\\ 3,\\, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
